Q1. What are Control Structures in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

Control structures are programming constructs that control the flow
of execution of a PL/SQL program.

They allow the program to make decisions and execute statements
repeatedly based on conditions.

Key Points

• Controls program flow.
• Implements business logic.
• Avoids repetitive code.
• Makes programs dynamic.

Types

1. Decision Making Statements
2. Looping Statements

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need Control Structures?

Interview Ready Answer

Control structures are used to make decisions and repeat a block of
code based on business requirements.

Uses

• Implement business logic.
• Execute code conditionally.
• Repeat tasks automatically.
• Reduce duplicate code.
• Improve readability.

Real-Life Example

ATM Machine

IF balance >= withdrawal amount

→ Allow withdrawal

ELSE

→ Show "Insufficient Balance"

======================================================================

Q3. What are Decision Making Statements?

Interview Ready Answer

Decision making statements execute different blocks of code based on
whether a condition is true or false.

Types

• IF
• IF-ELSE
• IF-ELSIF
• CASE

Purpose

• Choose one execution path.
• Execute code conditionally.

======================================================================

Q4. What are Looping Statements?

Interview Ready Answer

Looping statements repeatedly execute a block of code until a
condition becomes false or for a fixed number of iterations.

Types

• Simple LOOP
• WHILE LOOP
• FOR LOOP

Purpose

• Repeat code.
• Reduce duplicate statements.
• Automate repetitive tasks.

======================================================================

Q5. Difference between Decision Making Statements and Looping
Statements.

Interview Ready Answer

Decision Making Statements

• Used to make decisions.
• Executes one matching block.
• Based on conditions.

Examples

• IF
• IF-ELSE
• CASE

------------------------------------------------------------

Looping Statements

• Used to repeat execution.
• Executes the same block multiple times.
• Stops after condition becomes false or loop completes.

Examples

• LOOP
• WHILE LOOP
• FOR LOOP

======================================================================

Q6. Difference between IF and CASE.

Interview Ready Answer

IF

• Used for simple and complex conditions.
• Supports logical operators.
• Suitable for range-based conditions.
• More flexible.

------------------------------------------------------------

CASE

• Used when comparing one expression with multiple values.
• More readable than multiple IF-ELSE statements.
• Automatically exits after executing the matching branch.
• Does NOT require BREAK.

======================================================================

Q7. What are the Looping Statements available in PL/SQL?

Interview Ready Answer

PL/SQL provides three looping statements.

1. Simple LOOP
2. WHILE LOOP
3. FOR LOOP

======================================================================

Q8. Difference between FOR LOOP and WHILE LOOP.

Interview Ready Answer

FOR LOOP

• Number of iterations is known.
• Iterates over a predefined range.
• Counter is managed automatically.

------------------------------------------------------------

WHILE LOOP

• Number of iterations is unknown.
• Executes until condition becomes false.
• Condition is checked before every iteration.

======================================================================

Q9. When should we use FOR LOOP?

Interview Ready Answer

FOR LOOP should be used when the number of iterations is known in
advance.

Examples

• Print numbers from 1 to 100.
• Process fixed number of records.
• Fixed range iteration.

======================================================================

Q10. When should we use WHILE LOOP?

Interview Ready Answer

WHILE LOOP should be used when the number of iterations is not known
in advance and execution depends on a condition.

Examples

• Read data until user exits.
• Process records until no data remains.
• Execute until a condition becomes false.

